---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The overlay system"
  description="The DS only has 4 MB of main RAM. The game gets around that by loading and unloading 180 chunks of code as you play."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Overlays' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The game's code is split into two pieces. There's a big core
    binary called the <strong>ARM9</strong> (about 723 KB once it's
    decompressed) that's always in memory, and there are
    <strong>180 overlays</strong> that get swapped in and out as
    needed. Most overlays are between a few KB and about 130 KB.
  </p>

  <h2>What's always loaded</h2>
  <p>
    The ARM9 binary holds everything the game needs all the time:
    the content cache, the file loader, the sprite construction code,
    the game-state struct, the math library. About 5,000 functions in
    total.
  </p>

  <p>
    On top of the ARM9, two specific overlays are loaded almost
    continuously:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ov137</strong> — the rendering engine. About 800
      functions, mostly to do with building OAM lists, managing
      sprite slots, and copying data to video memory at vertical
      blank.</li>
    <li><strong>ov138</strong> — the main Tongari engine. Shares RAM
      with ov137 in the alternate slot; loaded for game modes that
      need 3D rendering. About 1,300 functions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Everything else is loaded on demand.
  </p>

  <h2>Shared RAM slots</h2>
  <p>
    Overlays don't each get their own address — they share. Several
    overlays might all be assigned the same RAM slot, with the
    understanding that only one of them is loaded at a time. The big
    shared slots are:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>RAM address</th><th>Slot purpose</th><th># overlays</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0x02155A00</code></td><td>Game mode controllers</td><td>66</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021597A0</code></td><td>Screen body renderers</td><td>25</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0215C540</code></td><td>Game-mode logic</td><td>20</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021FB3C0</code></td><td>Misc shared</td><td>18</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    66 overlays sharing one RAM slot means the game has 66 distinct
    "game modes" or screens that get treated as mutually exclusive.
    Only one of them is in memory at any given moment.
  </p>

  <h2>The controller-and-body pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Most screens in the game use two overlays in tandem: a
    <strong>controller</strong> in one shared slot, a <strong>body</strong>
    in another.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>The controller is the state machine. "Show the buy-item screen.
      Wait for input. The player picked an item, so update the
      selection. The player pressed B, so leave." It doesn't draw
      anything itself.</li>
    <li>The body is the renderer. It has a table of sprite placements
      and the code to walk that table and ask the rendering engine to
      put each sprite on the screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The shop screen is the cleanest example. The controller is ov003
    and the body is ov070. ov003 loads when you walk up to a shop.
    ov003 loads ov070 into its renderer slot and calls its init
    function. ov070 reads its 10-entry placement table and stamps the
    sprites onto the screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters for translating</h2>
  <p>
    Every time we change something the game reads — a path string, a
    text label hard-coded in an overlay, a menu item — we have to
    figure out which binary or overlay holds it. The ARM9 binary holds
    most of the "system" strings (item menu actions like "Hold,"
    "Place"). Overlay 96 holds the 20 NPC interaction menu options.
    Some overlays hold a single string each. Finding where a given
    string lives means searching across the ARM9 plus all 180
    overlays, then figuring out the byte budget around it (how much
    room is reserved before the next thing in memory).
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/`}>translation
    canon</a> section covers what we've nailed down for these
    fixed-width slots.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
